      "math_academy_id": "q-115018",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/5b3e36d9b39fcc8d61fe30219d2f3191a1b305a44995b27d5daddb5f18926e02.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(x-2)-4?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-2)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/482f1dc55995a299b21894c0ec191c0f8fb8213038cdc5f80da1e77a0d64af4e.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/a2832ebdf82e2a7bfddccf24fd598758e1ce86fc0726f9c128503574b09fcd0b.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2)-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/9473b7ebfbe7db3d1cfefcf3f7b616ffec4b9ad2e685a6fc40f4560e8d2a1c59.png)",
